Date:     Mon, 17 May 93 8:46:24 GMT
From: starr@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Subject: Currency Competition

Taking an ecological approach to currency competition is informative.  Some
comments:

The proposal to have fiat monies compete internationally as was suggested
in the Scientific American piece originated, I've heard, with "Denationali-
sation of Money," by F.A. Hayek, which he published after he got the
Nobel Prize in Economics for his work on the theory of the business cycle
with Ludwig von Mises in the late '20s and early '30s.

That book got an academic economist named Lawrence White interested in the
subject of free banking and led him to study it.  This resulted in some
published articles arguing in favor of free banking which are collected in
the book "Competition and Currency."  I haven't read it yet, but it's on
my list after Selgin's "Theory of Free Banking."

Last night at Free Exchange Lois Allen, a banker at Wells Fargo and econ
grad student at Golden Gate University, spoke about the history of free
banking - largely drawing on "The Experience of Free Banking," edited by
Kevin Dowd.  I did read some of the chapters of it and found them very
interesting.

Historically, free banking did lead to competition of diverse currencies,
but standardization occurred along local geographical lines and along
units of account.  For example, US and Canadian dollars tended not to
compete with each other very much except along the borders the two
countries shared as they still do.  Bank notes tended to circulate in the
areas where the banks that issued them were located, although clearinghouses
did emerge.

This leads me to conclude that if all the EC currencies were to compete
with each other, that the tendencies would be for the local currencies to
be stable, unless banks were widely permitted to engage in multinational
branching - I don't know what the case is now.  The main change I'd see
coming about is greater forex speculation.  That is, a wider distribution
of people would exchange foreign currencies as a matter of routine.  This
wouldn't be as restricted to professionals.

Of course, I don't know too much about this, so please correct me where I
go wrong.

Gresham's law would make some currencies disappear if all were made legal
tender; would the same thing happen if legal tender laws were abolished?

