Date: Mon, 17 May 1993 11:46:49 -0500 (EST)
From: KMOSTA01@ULKYVX.LOUISVILLE.EDU
Subject: PHIL: The Utility of Mathematics

Banach-Tarski paradox is in Natanson's "Theory of functions of a real variable".
There is no contradiction involved. The point of the Banach-Tarski paradox is  
that because the Axiom of Choice allows such complete flexibility with 
picking elements, you simply pick them to do the job -- make two spheres 
out of one. But ... all five pieces will not be measurable (Lebesgue
measurable), so no contradiction results. In fact, I believe Sierpinski
developed a model of the real line without the Axiom of Choice in which
all sets are measurable. Thus any construction of a nonmeasurable set
(the two most popular ones are due to Bernstein and Vitali) will require
the use of the Axiom of Choice. 

If you want to read more fun stuff about such weird constructions, one fun
book to read may be "The Scottish Book" edited by Daniel Mauldin (Math. Dept.
University of North Texas) -- it contains the problems posed an solved by
Banach and friends while drinking at the Scottish Cafe in Lwow, including
the famous Goose Problem posed by Mazur, solved by Per Enflo, for which
Mazur promised and delivered (in 1973, I believe) a goose. The problem was:
Does every separable Banach space have a Schauder basis? The answer is: NO.

By the way, I also do not find anything strange about Godel's Theorem. 
Let me present the story. So we admit there is actual infinity.
And by the way, two natural infinities are therefore: the one corresponding
to the number of objects in the set of natural numbers: {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, ...}
and the one corresponding to the set of lengths of objects -- lengths are
called real numbers, and include such things as e, pi, square root of two,
etc., all of which have infinite decimal expansions and can't be written
as fractions. Cantor showed that the second of the two infinities is bigger.
We call them aleph-naught and continuum, respectively. The Continuum
Hypothesis states that continuum is the cardinal number (a number describing
how big an infinity is) following aleph-not (that immediate successor of
aleph-not is called aleph-one), i.e., continuum = aleph-one. 
The answer to the hypothesis is: as you like it. I talk about this, because
it is, in a sense, similar to Godel's Theorem. Since nothing in our
experience gives us any indication of the nature of infinity, infinity
is a construct introduced rather arbitrarily (the strongest argument for it
is that every natural number is followed by its successor, but ... so what?
Mycielski's system has that axiom, yet no infinity), when we introduce it,
we must specify its nature (Continuum Hypothesis), and we must expect it
to remain somewhat beyong our reach (Godel's Theorem), because we never
experience infinity.

Another good references for this would probably be: Kuratowski's
"Set Theory" (I am almost certain it was translated to English) and
Jech's "Set Theory" (this one was written in English, Jech is a professor
at the University of Pennsylvania).

Natanson's book was translated to English, and it has a chapter on
cardinal and ordinal numbers, followed by the Banach-Tarski construction.

Krzys'

