>meaningfully be described as "random".  Care to elaborate, or hand me a
>ponter?
>-- 
>                                        Eric S. Raymond <esr@snark.thyrsus.com>


The most readable introduction is in John Casti's excellent "Searching for
Certainty: What Scientists Can Know About the Future," William Morrow and
Company, 1990. This book, by the author of "Paradigms Lost," is now out in
paperback and is great reading for all Extropian-minded folks.

I tired scanning and OCRing the 3-page description, but my OCR program
(TypeReader) kept crashing. Maybe I'll try again later.

Chaitin's own "Algorithmic Information Theory," Cambridge U. Press, 1987,
is much more dense and hard to read. Even Chaitin has called it "like a
sprint to the top of atall mountain," as opposed to a more leisurely stroll
or hike.

The "thermodynamics of information" (Chaitin's term, echoing also the work
of Landauer and Bennett, with whom Chaitin works at IBM), logic, tiling
spaces with logical tiles, and the incomputable are major dilletantish
interests of mine, so I welcome the discussion.

-Tim May

