Date: Mon, 17 May 1993 04:49:12 -0700 (PDT)
From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: Currency Competition

Tim Starr:
> Historically, free banking did lead to competition of diverse currencies,
> but standardization occurred along local geographical lines and along
> units of account.

This may be due to the transaction costs of a retailer having to
count, make change for, and convert multiple currencies.  With
today's electronic systems, we may be able to greatly lower
these costs:

(1) cash registers that can ring up purchases in several different
currencies.  They use the latest exchange quotes to convert from
the proprietor's to the customer's prefered currency.
(2) multi-currency credit cards
(3) automatic cash-sorting machines that can be programmed to
sort and count several kinds of paper bills
(4) digital cash

#3 still sounds somewhat costly, so true competition combined with
private transactions may have to wait for digital cash.  With less
concern for privacy, we can have competition between currencies
by allowing multi-currency credit cards.  The gas station may
take yen, the grocer Canadian dollars, and the copy shop FRN's,
but they all accept the credit card which automatically gets charged
with the proprietor's prefered currency.  The credit card bill is
denominated and paid in the customer's preferred currency, with
the exchange rate at the time of transaction used for conversion.

The delay between transaction and billing makes necessary currency
hedge positions for the credit card issuer. The transaction conversions 
can be bundled into a synthetic asset and traded on the money market,
so that the money market and not the credit card issuer assumes the 
exchange rate risk.

